A||N||9||87ECO5||Buy or lease a new vehicle - opinions wanted||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||07-23-2001||04:18 PM||||I ran a search to see if this has come up before.  Found a few things on breaking out of lease contracts early but nothing about the general feeling of which is better.<P>So, looking for opinions (also pros and cons of each) on the age-old question:  Buy or Lease?||||reg||5
Z||000001||JSocha||07-23-2001||04:26 PM||||I always buy my vehicles outright.  Less costly in the long run as I get FULL WARRANTY.<P>Lets see though, if you only want to have the vehicle for a year or two, fully warrantied and don't put that many miles on then get a new one, Lease.<P>If you are going to have it for a few years or going to be putting on the miles, buy.<P>Again, this is just me, I have either always purchased my vehicles outright or on a six year loan and to me that is cheaper then shelling out money for the rest of my life, especially if I can pay for the vehicle sooner.<P>"Know what I mean Vern?"<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||DecadenceR||07-23-2001||04:41 PM||||it all depends on your personal driving habbits and needs. i used to work for a car dealer, and they really screw some people with leasing. If you're not going to be using it a lot, and you want to get rid of it after a few years, then I'd say yeah, lease. Just remember all the penalties for over mileage, any beauty imperfactions, and mechanical wear. Sometimes it costs more to turn in the car than you woudl think. However, as I'm sure you already know, lease payments average $100 less than financing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||07-23-2001||06:45 PM||||buy used only never new<BR>remember what PT said "there one born every minute" thats who to buy from not the pro's<BR>find the fool who must sell it now and don't know the market, dealers never sell as cheap as a fool.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||Uaana||07-23-2001||08:39 PM||||What Ray B said.<BR>Leases are a bloody pain in the arse. <BR>And if you are willing to pay 20k+ for a car just to drive it off the lot and have it valued 4k+ less than what you just paid for it.<BR>The best deal overall is buying one 1yr old. <BR>These are usually lease returns. Watch for rental returns! But at least you get the balance of the warranty without the huge hit in depreciation.||||reg||1
Z||000005||AusFiero||07-24-2001||04:09 AM||||I don't know about your tax advantages over their eco but over here it seems to work this way. I am a small business, I buy my vehicles outright for best tax advantages. My bro inlaw owns a large corporation, they lease for best tax advantages. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000006||frontal lobe||07-25-2001||01:33 AM||||If you put less than 12,000 miles per year (for sure 15,000/year), and you plan to keep it less than 3 years, definitely lease.<P>If you are going to keep it about 4 years, probably still lease, but it's very close.<P>If you are willing to keep it 5 or more years, definitely buy it.<P>For your over-all lowest cost, buy a year or two old car, like rayb says, and then keep it over 5 years.<P>If you are going to keep buying or leasing new and trading every 2-3 years, that is going to be your highest cost either way.  But if you have the money to blow and love having the newest car, go for it.<P>What are you thinking about getting??||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||07-25-2001||09:31 AM||||Remember, when the lease is up you can walk away from the car.  Of course you have to because you ain't got no wheels no mo.<P>Leases get you into a trap.  Less money up front no car at the end.<P>If you find a "good" car drive it for 150000 miles.<P>When buying, keep the term as short as possible 3 years is best, four years is break even. Longer than that, the car is worth less than what you owe on it.  Of course, the cars in todays market are so bloody expensive, and salaries haven't kept pace with car cost inflation, you can only do the lease option.  Buying the car outright after the lease, the dealers even screw you, with all the fees etc.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||87ECO5||07-25-2001||10:08 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>What are you thinking about getting??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Haven't really thought about it, to be honest.<P>I feel like I made a costly mistake with the purchase of our 1994 Nissan Pathfinder.  We bought it in 1999 with 75,000 miles and financed it for 4.5 years.  Long story short - I needed something to haul my boat and I fell in love with it when I saw it.  Unfortunately, I couldn't afford the monthly payments of a 2 or 3 year loan.  Hey, I never said I was good with money!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Today (a little over two years after the purchase), the truck has over 102,000 miles on it and we still have another 2.5 years to go on the loan.  Though it's been a perfect vehicle so far [knocking on wood!!!], I'm concerned that it will begin to nickel and dime us to death.<P>I saw a lease on a new Civic LX (power goodies, A/C, CD) for $189/month for three years.  Certainly not a comparable replacement for an SUV but, at about half the monthly payment of the Pathfinder, definitely something we could live with for a couple of years while we socked money away ... and the repairs would be covered.<P>There's not much I hate more than having to pay for repairs on a vehicle that I'm making monthly payments on!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 07-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||JSocha||07-25-2001||10:33 AM||||Here is a little money tip for you to consider and to pay your loans off faster, reduce the interest you otherwise would be paying and at the same time, still making your monthly payment (regardless if it is a home, auto or other loan as it works for all of them).<P>Lets say you have taken out a loan and the monthly payments are $300.00 for the term and interest rate you have taken it out at.<P>Well, you could pay religiously the one lump sum payment at the end of the month when it is due of $300.00.  Loan instutitions and banks love this and will not tell you the following as they are trying to make money via the interest you are paying.<P>How can you reduce the term and/or the interest you ineveitably pay over the time period?<P>Simple and this is what they do not want you to know.<P>Taking that $300.00 payment you have to make per month, I'm going to divide it simply by 2 thus giving me $150.00.  Or what is equivelant to two payments per month of $150.00.<P>In the first two weeks, send in the first $150.00 dollars, which gets applied to your loan and reduces the principle you are paying interest on.  Then when it comes due, send in the remaining $150.00 which also gets applied to the loan thus meeting your $300.00 monthly payment obligation.<P>By doing this, you are effectively reducing your overall interest payment that you would incurr over the term period (thats money in your pocket in the long run), reducing your debt and paying your loans off faster.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Just a tip from someone who does this all the time and something I have learned in investment strategy seminars and from tapes and books.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||12
